---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
---

<Layout>
  <main>
    <section class="case-study">
      <div class="container case-study-layout">
        <aside class="case-study-aside">
          <p class="section-tag">
            Case Study
          </p>

          <h2>
            A voice-first AI concierge that turns natural-language dining preferences into reservations.
          </h2>

          <p>
            How I designed DineWise, a conversational AI agent that transforms the way people
            discover and book restaurants through Swiggy Dineout.
          </p>

          <div class="impact-strip">
            <div>
              <strong>Voice-first</strong>
              <span>conversational interaction</span>
            </div>
            <div>
              <strong>Top 3</strong>
              <span>matches spoken, not 20+</span>
            </div>
            <div>
              <strong>Real-time</strong>
              <span>Dineout availability & booking</span>
            </div>
          </div>
        </aside>

        <article class="case-study-story">
          <section>
            <span>01</span>
            <h3>Overview</h3>
            <p>
              DineWise is a voice-first AI concierge that transforms the way people discover
              and book restaurants. Instead of navigating multiple filters for cuisine, budget,
              ambience, and availability, users simply describe the experience they want in
              natural language.
            </p>
            <blockquote>
              "I'm meeting an old friend this weekend. We want somewhere relaxed where we can
              actually talk, with good food and a budget of around ₹1,500 for two."
            </blockquote>
            <p>
              The AI interprets the user's intent, searches restaurants through Swiggy Dineout
              MCP, checks real-time availability, and completes the reservation through a
              conversational experience.
            </p>
          </section>

          <section>
            <span>02</span>
            <h3>The Problem</h3>
            <p>
              Restaurant booking apps are optimized around filters, but people rarely think in
              filters. Users usually know:
            </p>

            <ul>
              <li>The occasion (date, family dinner, catch-up).</li>
              <li>The atmosphere they want.</li>
              <li>Their budget.</li>
              <li>Preferred timing.</li>
            </ul>

            <p>
              What they don't know is which restaurant best fits those constraints. The friction
              isn't booking a table - it's making the decision.
            </p>
          </section>

          <section>
            <span>03</span>
            <h3>The Solution</h3>
            <p>
              DineWise acts as a conversational decision-making layer on top of Swiggy Dineout.
              The core innovation is not voice itself - it's the ability to convert unstructured
              human intent into structured restaurant recommendations.
            </p>
          </section>

          <section>
            <span>04</span>
            <h3>User Journey</h3>

            <dl>
              <div>
                <dt>Express intent naturally</dt>
                <dd>"I'm meeting an old friend this weekend…"</dd>
              </div>
              <div>
                <dt>AI understands context</dt>
                <dd>Extracts occasion, ambience, budget, party size, and timing.</dd>
              </div>
              <div>
                <dt>Search Dineout</dt>
                <dd>Retrieves restaurants, offers, and available reservation slots.</dd>
              </div>
              <div>
                <dt>Recommend & reserve</dt>
                <dd>Explains why a restaurant matches before booking with confirmation.</dd>
              </div>
            </dl>
          </section>

          <section>
            <span>05</span>
            <h3>Designing for Voice, Not Chat</h3>
            <p>
              A key product decision was optimizing responses specifically for voice interactions.
            </p>

            <dl>
              <div>
                <dt>Traditional search</dt>
                <dd>Displays 20+ restaurants and expects users to compare filters manually.</dd>
              </div>
              <div>
                <dt>DineWise</dt>
                <dd>Speaks only the top 3 matches and explains why the best option fits the user's intent.</dd>
              </div>
            </dl>

            <p>
              This reduces cognitive load and makes the interaction feel like talking to a
              knowledgeable concierge rather than querying a database.
            </p>
          </section>

          <section>
            <span>06</span>
            <h3>Key Product Decisions</h3>

            <dl>
              <div>
                <dt>Conversational constraint extraction</dt>
                <dd>
                  The agent identifies implicit preferences like "somewhere we can actually talk"
                  and maps them to ambience rather than treating it as a keyword search.
                </dd>
              </div>
              <div>
                <dt>Re-planning instead of restarting</dt>
                <dd>
                  If the user says "Actually, we'd prefer outdoor seating," the agent preserves
                  budget, party size, and timing while updating only the changed preference.
                </dd>
              </div>
              <div>
                <dt>Transparent recommendations</dt>
                <dd>
                  Every recommendation includes 2–3 factual reasons (budget match, ambience,
                  availability) instead of exposing internal reasoning.
                </dd>
              </div>
            </dl>
          </section>

          <section>
            <span>07</span>
            <h3>Technology</h3>

            <dl>
              <div>
                <dt>AI Agent</dt>
                <dd>Llama 3.2 (local via Ollama).</dd>
              </div>
              <div>
                <dt>Voice</dt>
                <dd>Browser Speech-to-Text + Text-to-Speech.</dd>
              </div>
              <div>
                <dt>Commerce Layer</dt>
                <dd>Swiggy Dineout MCP.</dd>
              </div>
              <div>
                <dt>Frontend</dt>
                <dd>React, TypeScript, and Tailwind CSS.</dd>
              </div>
            </dl>
          </section>

          <section>
            <span>08</span>
            <h3>Outcome</h3>
            <p>
              DineWise demonstrates how AI agents can improve decision-making in commerce, not
              just automate transactions. Rather than replacing restaurant search, it reimagines
              the discovery experience as a natural conversation that understands intent, adapts
              to changing preferences, and completes reservations through Swiggy's Dineout
              ecosystem.
            </p>
          </section>
        </article>
      </div>
    </section>
  </main>
</Layout>


<style>
.case-study {
  padding:120px 0 80px;
}

.case-study-layout {
  display:grid;
  grid-template-columns:minmax(280px, 0.9fr) minmax(0, 1.35fr);
  gap:72px;
  align-items:start;
}

.case-study-aside {
  position:sticky;
  top:110px;
}

.case-study-aside h2 {
  margin-bottom:22px;
}

.case-study-aside p {
  color:var(--text-secondary);
  max-width:520px;
}

.impact-strip {
  display:grid;
  grid-template-columns:repeat(3, 1fr);
  border:1px solid var(--border);
  margin-top:36px;
}

.impact-strip div {
  padding:20px;
  border-right:1px solid var(--border);
}

.impact-strip div:last-child {
  border-right:0;
}

.impact-strip strong {
  display:block;
  font-size:1.5rem;
  color:var(--text-primary);
  margin-bottom:6px;
}

.impact-strip span {
  color:var(--text-secondary);
  font-size:0.85rem;
}

.case-study-story {
  border-left:1px solid var(--border);
  padding-left:40px;
}

.case-study-story section {
  position:relative;
  padding-bottom:56px;
}

.case-study-story section:last-child {
  padding-bottom:0;
}

.case-study-story section > span {
  display:inline-block;
  color:var(--accent);
  font-size:0.85rem;
  font-weight:700;
  margin-bottom:12px;
}

.case-study-story h3 {
  font-size:1.45rem;
  margin-bottom:14px;
}

.case-study-story p {
  color:var(--text-secondary);
  margin-bottom:16px;
}

.case-study-story ul {
  color:var(--text-secondary);
  padding-left:18px;
}

.case-study-story li {
  margin-bottom:10px;
}

.case-study-story blockquote {
  border-left:3px solid var(--accent);
  padding:4px 0 4px 20px;
  margin:0 0 16px;
  color:var(--text-primary);
  font-style:italic;
}

.flow-line {
  display:flex;
  flex-wrap:wrap;
  gap:10px;
  margin-bottom:24px;
}

.flow-line span {
  border:1px solid var(--border);
  padding:10px 14px;
  color:var(--text-primary);
  background:var(--bg-secondary);
}

.case-study-story dl {
  display:grid;
  gap:18px;
  margin-top:22px;
}

.case-study-story dl div {
  border-top:1px solid var(--border);
  padding-top:16px;
}

.case-study-story dt {
  color:var(--text-primary);
  font-weight:700;
  margin-bottom:6px;
}

.case-study-story dd {
  color:var(--text-secondary);
}

@media(max-width:800px){

  .case-study {
    padding:100px 0 60px;
  }

  .case-study-layout {
    grid-template-columns:1fr;
    gap:44px;
  }

  .case-study-aside {
    position:static;
  }

  .impact-strip {
    grid-template-columns:1fr;
  }

  .impact-strip div {
    border-right:0;
    border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);
  }

  .impact-strip div:last-child {
    border-bottom:0;
  }

  .case-study-story {
    border-left:0;
    padding-left:0;
  }

}

@media(max-width:480px){

  .case-study {
    padding:90px 0 60px;
  }

}

@media(max-height:800px) and (min-width:801px){

  .case-study {
    padding-top:96px;
  }

  .case-study-aside {
    top:96px;
  }

}
</style>
